  {
   "id": "r10",
   "label": "plain_text",
   "bbox": {
    "x0": 90,
    "y0": 627,
    "x1": 914,
    "y1": 1144
   },
   "text": "\"The Mangbetu excel in their iron work; indeed, their well-forged and finely worked knives are masterpieces of Negro blacksmithing. Their pottery in its best samples reminds one of ancient Greek work. In producing well-balanced forms of artistic finish they show a very high development. Their carved and ornamented stools, benches, figures and shields, and their hatpins of ivory may well be classed among works of art.\"\n\n\"That is, these Negroes compare well in their artistic culture with the Greeks of a period not long before Pericles, and are far superior to the Germans and Gauls before Roman civilization reached them.\"\n\n. A. C. P.",
   "status": "ok",
   "confidence": 0.975068986415863,
